<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Balaton Principality</h1><p class="st-meta">839 CE – 876 CE (approx.) · capital Mosapurc</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Frankish vassal principality around Lake Balaton where Pribina and Kocel built churches and welcomed Methodius's Slavonic liturgy.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribina">Pribina</a>, a Slavic prince expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nitra">Nitra</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mojmir-i">Mojmir I</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/great-moravia">Moravia</a> around 833, entered Frankish service. Around 839 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-german">Louis the German</a> granted him lands in Lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>, and he built a fortified seat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zalavar">Mosapurc</a> (Zalavár) on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zala-river">Zala</a> river near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-balaton">Lake Balaton</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribina">Pribina</a> ruled as a Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, settling <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> and founding some 30 churches consecrated by the archbishops of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzburg">Salzburg</a>. Under his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kocel">Kocel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zalavar">Mosapurc</a> was an important church centre. In 867 Kocel welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-and-methodius">Cyril and Methodius</a> and supported the Slavonic liturgy, and Methodius was made archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> in 870.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pribina">Pribina</a> was killed fighting Moravians in 861. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kocel">Kocel's</a> death around 876 the principality returned to direct Frankish rule, and the Hungarian conquest of the 890s ended Frankish power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/balaton-principality.jpg" alt="Balaton Principality" width="320" height="352"><figcaption>Image: Koryakov Yuri · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Slav-7-8-obrez.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>839 CE – 876 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Mosapurc</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Pribina, Kocel</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Slavonic; Old High German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>, <a href="/countries/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a href="/countries/slovenia">Slovenia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Lower Pannonia, Pribina's principality</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=858&amp;focus=balaton-principality" aria-label="Balaton Principality on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5132 528 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Balaton Principality lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5199 691h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5452 784h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5452 784h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Hungary. <a href="/atlas?year=858&amp;focus=balaton-principality">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
